{
  "requirement": "Usage MUST record mandatory input counts and sizes by evidence kind, in UTF-8\nbytes and lines, and the same information for additional inputs requested through\n`habeas` or `ripgrep`. A requirements input MUST also record its number of matched declarations.\nUsage MUST distinguish unique inputs from repeated reads, successful from\nfailed reads, and prepared inputs from those actually sent to the model.\nFile-discovery calls MUST count as tool calls without recording directory\nlistings as evidence or source inputs.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Verdict: the requirement is met.** The {impl} code covers each obligation, and the tests exercise the design space. I did not run the tests.\n\n**Recording sizes by evidence kind (`UsageLog::prepared`, src/authoring/usage.rs:132)**\n- Each mandatory input, and each tool-delivered input, becomes an `InputUsage` entry.\n- The entry records:\n  - `kind`: `outline` if the input has `item`, `requirements` if it has `requirements`, otherwise `definition`.\n  - `origin`: `mandatory`, `habeas` or `ripgrep`.\n  - `bytes`: UTF-8 bytes of the text lines plus the newlines between them.\n  - `lines`: the number of text lines.\n- `configured_record` (src/authoring/mod.rs:337) calls `usage.prepared(\"mandatory\", …)` and saves the log.\n- `assess` calls `usage.prepared(<tool name>, &delivery.inputs)` for each successful tool call. That covers the additional inputs requested through `habeas` or `ripgrep`, including the term definitions delivered with them.\n- There is no aggregate count per kind. Counts per kind come from the per-input entries, which I consider enough.\n\n**Requirements inputs and matched declarations**\n- `components` is the length of `matches`, and 1 for other kinds.\n- The unit test src/authoring/usage.rs:363 asserts `components == 2` for a requirements input.\n\n**Unique versus repeated**\n- `unique` comes from a `seen` set keyed on the full input value.\n- The unit test asserts a repeated `habeas` read is `unique == false`.\n- The CLI test (tests/authoring_cli.rs:232) asserts the same for a repeated read.\n\n**Successful versus failed reads**\n- `assess` calls `usage.tool(name, failed)` for every call, so failures are counted per tool in `tool_failures`.\n- Failed reads record no input.\n- The CLI test has a missing-file `habeas` call and asserts `tool_failures.habeas == 1`.\n- The unit test src/authoring/usage.rs:557 covers failure and skipped counting.\n\n**Prepared versus sent to the model**\n- `begin_request` stamps `submitted_in_request` on every input that does not have one yet.\n- `end_request` stamps `sent_in_request` only when the request returned usage, meaning it completed.\n- Tests:\n  - The unit test at line 363 checks null, then submitted, then sent.\n  - The unit test at line 588 shows a failed first attempt leaving `sent` null and a later retry setting it.\n  - The throttled-provider test in src/authoring/mod.rs:637 covers 8 failures: `submitted_in_request == 0` and `sent_in_request == 8`.\n  - The CLI test asserts `sent_in_request == 1` for a tool-delivered input.\n\n**File discovery**\n- `list_files` returns `inputs: Vec::new()`, so `prepared` records nothing for it.\n- `assess` still calls `usage.tool(\"list_files\", …)`, so it counts toward `tool_call_count`.\n- The CLI test counts 6 tool calls, which includes `list_files`.\n- `tools.rs` tests assert that listings have empty inputs.\n\n**Gaps**\n- I found no unmarked implementation. The `components` and `origin` fields are part of the stated recording.\n- The persisted log is content-free: the tests assert that no source text, paths or selectors appear in it.\n- I found no FIXME or other acknowledged gaps.",
